course: everyday
lesson: 123
title: Greeting neighbours and new people
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Friendly neighbours look out for each other, so you say hello or wave when you pass them, and a kind word about their garden builds good feeling on the street.
explain: An elderly neighbour who lives alone is cheered by being noticed, and with your parents you can check that they are all right in very hot, cold or stormy weather.
explain: When you meet someone new, you look at them, smile and say "Nice to meet you"; if you forget their name, you simply ask again, because it happens to everyone.
explain: A ball over the fence is a small test of manners: you do not climb into the garden, which is trespassing, but knock, say sorry and ask politely.
explain: A boy in Colorado sees a new girl join his class; he tells her his name and offers to show her where the lunch room is, and her scary first day becomes much easier.
fact: Friendly neighbours look out for each other and help when someone needs it.
fact: Moving house is hard, and a welcome, like a card or some biscuits, helps a new family feel at home.
fact: People like to hear their own name, so it is worth learning your neighbours' names.
fact: Lonely older people are cheered by being noticed, and a check in bad weather may help in an emergency.
fact: Going into someone's garden without asking is rude and is trespassing.
fact: When you are introduced to someone, you look at them, smile and say "Nice to meet you".
fact: Starting at a new school is scary, and a friendly face helps a lot.
fact: It is fine to ask how to say a name that is hard for you, and saying it properly shows respect.
fact: If you forget a name, you say "Sorry, could you tell me your name again?"
fact: A smile and "Hi" is enough to start when you feel shy, and the other person may be shy too.
exercise: You pass your neighbour in the street. What do you do? | Say hello or wave | Look the other way and walk faster | Wait for them to greet you first | Cross the road to avoid them
exercise: A new family moves in next door. What is a kind thing to do? | With your parents, say hello and welcome them, maybe with a card | Watch them from the window for a few days but never say a word to them | Wait a year to see if they are nice | Ask them to keep away from your garden
exercise: Your neighbour is working in the garden as you go past. What can you say? | Hello, and maybe something nice about the garden | Nothing, because gardeners do not like to be disturbed | That their garden looks messier than yours | Ask them to pick you some flowers
exercise: You are not sure of your neighbour's name. What do you do? | Ask your parents and use the name next time | Call them "Hey, neighbour" every time so you never get it wrong | Make up a name that sounds right | Never greet them, to avoid a mistake
exercise: An elderly neighbour lives alone. How can you help? | Greet them and, with your parents, check on them in bad weather | Leave them alone, because old people prefer to be ignored | Go into their house whenever you like to check that they are all right | Only talk to them on their birthday
exercise: Your ball goes over into the neighbour's garden. What should you do? | Knock on their door, say sorry and ask politely to get it back | Climb over the fence quickly before anyone sees | Leave it there and buy a new ball | Shout over the fence again and again until someone throws it back
exercise: Your parents introduce you to a new person. What do you do? | Look at them, smile and say "Nice to meet you" | Look at the floor and wait for them to leave | Say "Who are you?" | Give a small nod and walk away
exercise: A new pupil joins your class. What is kind? | Say hello, tell them your name and offer to show them around | Wait to see if the other children like them first, then decide | Stare at them so they know they are new | Tell them the school is very strict
exercise: You meet a boy whose name is hard for you to say. What is respectful? | Ask him to say it again and try your best | Give him an easier nickname instead so nobody has to try | Avoid saying his name at all | Say it any way you like
exercise: You forget a girl's name right after being introduced. What do you do? | Say "Sorry, could you tell me your name again?" | Avoid her for the rest of the day | Call her by the first name you think of and hope it is right | Pretend you never met her
exercise: You feel too shy to say anything to a new person. What is enough to start? | A smile and "Hi" | A long speech about yourself | Nothing, until they speak to you | A joke about their clothes
exercise: Why is climbing into a neighbour's garden for your ball wrong? | Going in without asking is rude and is trespassing | Because children's balls are never allowed in other people's gardens | Because neighbours always keep the balls | Because only adults may touch a fence
test: A girl in Florida forgot the new teacher's name after one minute. What is the best thing to do? | Politely ask the teacher to tell her the name again | Call the teacher "Miss" for the rest of the year | Ask a friend to make up a name | Hope the teacher never asks her anything
test: Why is a hello to an old man who lives alone more important than it seems? | Being noticed cheers lonely people and shows if they need help | Because old people often pay children a little money just to say hello | Because he might be a famous person | Because it is a law to greet every neighbour
test: A new boy in Wellington stands alone at break on his first day. What would help him most? | Tell him your name and invite him to join your game | Watch from a distance to see if he finds some friends by himself | Tell him new pupils must wait a week to play | Ask him why his family moved
test: Your frisbee lands in the garden of a neighbour you do not know. What do you do? | Knock, say sorry and ask if you may get it | Jump the fence while they are out | Send your little brother over the fence | Forget it and never play near the fence again
test: Why do people like it when you use their name? | People like hearing their name, and it shows you remember them | Because names are secret and only close friends are allowed to know them | Because it is shorter than saying hello | Because everyone forgets their own name
test: A new girl's name is Siobhan, and you are not sure how to say it. What should you do? | Ask her how to say it and try your best | Call her "Sarah", since it is close enough | Never use her name to avoid a mistake | Spell it out loud letter by letter every time
test: A family with two small children moves in across the road in Arizona. Which is the kindest welcome? | Go over with your parents to say hello, maybe with a card | Wait at home until they come over and introduce themselves to you | Tell other neighbours they look strange | Leave a note asking them to be quiet
test: Both you and a new classmate are shy. Who should say "Hi" first? | You can, because a smile and "Hi" is enough to start | Nobody, because shy people prefer silence | Only the teacher is allowed to start a talk between two shy pupils | Whoever is taller should go first
test: forgot the neighbours name and she just said hi to me | Say sorry, could you tell me your name again | Pretend you know it and say hi back without using any name | Call her lady | Walk away fast
test: new fam moved in next door, can we do something | Take a card or some biscuits to welcome them | Wait until they come to you | Watch them from the window | Ask them to be quiet
test: old mr jones next door, havent seen him in ages | Tell a grown-up, he might need help | He's probably on holiday, forget it | Climb in his window to check | Post a note and do nothing else
test: cant say the new girls name right | Ask her how to say it | Give her an easier nickname | Avoid saying her name | Just say it any way you like
explain: A girl in Florida went into a new neighbour's house to see his puppy without telling her parents; nothing happened, but the family searched the street for an hour in panic.
explain: A boy in Leeds was rude to a new neighbour's child for being from another country; the families fell out, and he lost a friend he later wished he had.
fact: Children should never go into a house or car without their parent or carer knowing and agreeing.
fact: An adult who asks a child to keep a secret from their parents is a warning sign, so tell your parent anyway.
fact: Being welcoming to new people often leads to good friendships, while being rude can hurt for a long time.
test: new neighbour says come see the puppies inside | Ask your parent first, never go in without them knowing | Go in quickly, puppies are cute | Go in if he seems nice | Climb up to the window and look through it at the puppies instead
test: new kid on our street talks funny, gonna laugh at him | Say hi instead, laughing at people hurts | Laugh, it's just a joke | Copy his voice | Tell everyone to avoid him
test: neighbour i don't know wants me to keep a secret from mum | Tell your mum anyway, adults shouldn't ask kids for secrets | Keep it, you promised | Keep it if he gives you sweets, because then it's a fair deal for both | Tell only your friends
test: did u hear Lucy went in the new guy's house to see his puppy n "the whole town had helicopters out". what's probs exaggerated | The helicopters; her family really did search in a panic | That her family did not know where she was | That you should tell a parent before you go into anyone's house | That her parents were frightened
test: my bro says the new kid's family "talks funny so don't bother". real lesson? | Be welcoming; rudeness hurts and you lose a friend | Only make friends with people who come from your own street or town | Wait for the new kid to say hello first | Talk to them only if they speak like you
test: kids reckon old Mrs Grey's garden is "haunted" n dare each other to sneak in. should i go along with it | No, going in uninvited is trespassing and rude | Yes, if you are really quick and make sure that nobody sees you | Yes, it is only a game, so it is fine | Yes, if you leave something in return
exam: Identify the principal response appropriate upon being introduced to a person for the first time. | Look at them, smile and say "Nice to meet you" | Look away and wait for them to speak first | Ask them at once where they live and how old | Say nothing until a parent tells you to speak
exam: Whereas a person's name has been forgotten shortly after an introduction, state the appropriate course of action. | Say "Sorry, could you tell me your name again?" | Avoid the person until the name is remembered | Invent a nickname and use it in its place | Ask somebody else secretly for the name
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning entry into a neighbour's garden without permission. | It is rude and amounts to trespassing | It is permitted if a ball has gone in | It is acceptable when nobody is home | It is polite if one is very quiet
exam: Ascertain the condition under which a child may enter another person's house or car. | Only with a parent or carer knowing and agreeing | Whenever the person seems kind and friendly | Whenever there is a pet to be shown inside | Only if the house or car is on the same street
